# RiftRace Frontend Rules

## Stack
- Angular
- TypeScript strict mode
- Standalone components
- Angular Router
- Angular HttpClient

Use the Angular version already established by the project. Do not upgrade it casually.

## Architecture
Prefer feature-oriented structure:

- core/
- shared/
- features/auth/
- features/home/
- features/challenge/
- features/player/

Keep API access in services.
Do not put HTTP calls directly in templates/components unless there is a strong reason.

Keep business rules on the backend.
Angular handles presentation, user interaction and client-side UX.

## UX
The main challenge screen must be immediately understandable.

Prioritize:
- current LP;
- LP gained during the challenge;
- current rank;
- rank comparison;
- position in the challenge;
- participant/duo identity;
- challenge status;
- last data refresh;
- next available refresh.

Prefer visual indicators, cards, progress elements and clear ranking over large data tables.

## Challenge states
The challenge UI must clearly distinguish:
1. not started;
2. active;
3. finished, if implemented.

For a scheduled challenge, show the exact start date/time and a useful countdown when appropriate.

## Refresh UX
The challenge refresh control must:
- show whether refresh is available;
- show a countdown/next available time when blocked;
- provide feedback while refreshing;
- display errors clearly.

The UI must never assume that a refresh succeeded until the backend confirms it.

The frontend must not attempt to bypass the 2-minute backend restriction.

## Forms
Validate basic input client-side for UX, but always rely on backend validation for security and correctness.

For challenge creation:
- challenge type is required;
- start date/time is required;
- SoloQ participant limit is 16;
- DuoQ limit is 8 duos.

## Accessibility
Use semantic HTML.
Buttons must be real buttons.
Inputs need labels.
Do not communicate important state only through color.

## Responsive design
The main challenge visualization must work on desktop and mobile.
Avoid layouts that require horizontal scrolling for the primary challenge experience.
